---
Run these examples from the checkout in the
[ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/user-guide/quickstart')). The default CPU backend
exposes multiple logical devices on one host, so you can try both examples
without accelerator hardware.

# [Map tensor dimensions to devices]($heading.id('map-tensor-dimensions-to-devices'))

Follow a dense layer on a **simulated 2 × 2 logical mesh**. The two `.data`
groups split the batch. Within each group, two `.model` partitions split the hidden dimension.

```=html
<div class="sharding-explorer" data-sharding-explorer data-toc-parent="map-tensor-dimensions-to-devices">
  <div class="sharding-controls" hidden data-pagefind-ignore>
    <div class="sharding-heading"><h3 id="dense-layer" data-toc-label="Dense layer">Dense layer</h3><span>Select a tensor · shapes per device</span></div>
    <div class="sharding-equation" role="group" aria-label="Select a dense layer tensor">
      <button type="button" data-tensor="input"><strong>Input</strong> <small></small></button><span aria-hidden="true">×</span>
      <button type="button" data-tensor="weights"><strong>Weights</strong> <small></small></button><span aria-hidden="true" class="sharding-plus">+</span>
      <button type="button" data-tensor="bias"><strong>Bias</strong> <small></small></button><span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <button type="button" data-tensor="output"><strong>Output</strong> <small></small></button>
    </div>
    <div class="sharding-workspace">
      <div class="sharding-mesh-panel">
        <div class="sharding-heading"><h3 id="device-mesh" data-toc-label="Device mesh">Device mesh</h3><span>Select a coordinate</span></div>
        <div class="sharding-mesh" role="group" aria-label="Select a logical device by data and model coordinate">
          <span></span><span>model 0</span><span>model 1</span>
          <span>data 0</span>
          <button type="button" data-device="0"><span class="sharding-patch" aria-hidden="true"></span><code>(0, 0)</code><small></small></button>
          <button type="button" data-device="1"><span class="sharding-patch" aria-hidden="true"></span><code>(0, 1)</code><small></small></button>
          <span>data 1</span>
          <button type="button" data-device="2"><span class="sharding-patch" aria-hidden="true"></span><code>(1, 0)</code><small></small></button>
          <button type="button" data-device="3"><span class="sharding-patch" aria-hidden="true"></span><code>(1, 1)</code><small></small></button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sharding-inspector" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        <strong data-slice-title></strong>
        <dl class="sharding-shapes"><div><dt>Global shape</dt><dd data-global-shape></dd></div><div><dt>Local shape</dt><dd data-local-shape></dd></div></dl>
        <span class="sharding-patch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <code data-slice></code><p data-placement-note></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="command-shell sharding-code">
    <h3 id="dense-layer-in-zig" data-toc-label="Dense layer in Zig">Dense layer in Zig</h3>
    <span class="command-language icon-code">Zig · forward</span>
    <pre><code data-copy-label="Copy dense layer in Zig"><span data-line="input"><span class="token keyword">const</span> x = input.<span class="token function">convert</span>(.f32);</span>
<span data-line="weights"><span class="token keyword">var</span> y = x.<span class="token function">dot</span>(self.w, .feature);</span>
<span data-line="bias">y = y.<span class="token function">add</span>(self.b.<span class="token function">broad</span>(y.<span class="token function">shape</span>()));</span>
<span data-line="output">y = y.<span class="token function">withPartitioning</span>(.dp_mp, .{ .batch = .data, .hidden = .model });</span>
</code></pre>
  </div>
  <details class="sharding-rule">
    <summary>Partitioning<span data-rule-name></span></summary>
    <div data-rule="input"><pre><code class="zig" data-copy-label="Copy input partitioning">const input_shape = zml.Shape.init(.{ .batch = 16, .feature = 32 }, .f32)
    .withPartitioning(mesh, .{ .batch = .data, .feature = .replicated });
</code></pre></div>
    <div data-rule="weights"><pre><code class="zig" data-copy-label="Copy weight partitioning">const w_shape = zml.Shape.init(.{ .feature = 32, .hidden = 64 }, .f32)
    .withPartitioning(mesh, .{ .feature = .replicated, .hidden = .model });
</code></pre></div>
    <div data-rule="bias"><pre><code class="zig" data-copy-label="Copy bias partitioning">const b_shape = zml.Shape.init(.{ .hidden = 64 }, .f32)
    .withPartitioning(mesh, .{ .hidden = .model });
</code></pre></div>
    <div data-rule="output"><pre><code class="zig" data-copy-label="Copy output partitioning">y = y.withPartitioning(.dp_mp, .{ .batch = .data, .hidden = .model });
</code></pre></div>
  </details>
  <details class="sharding-text" open>
    <summary>All tensor placements on the 2 × 2 mesh</summary>
    <table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Tensor</th><th scope="col">Global shape</th><th scope="col">Per-device placement</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr data-placement="input"><th scope="row">Input</th><td><code>batch=<span data-dim="batch" data-split="data">16</span>, feature=<span data-dim="feature">32</span></code></td><td>Each data group holds eight batch rows with all 32 features. Both model partitions in that group receive the same input slice.</td></tr>
      <tr data-placement="weights"><th scope="row">Weights</th><td><code>feature=<span data-dim="feature">32</span>, hidden=<span data-dim="hidden" data-split="model">64</span></code></td><td>Each model partition holds 32 hidden columns with all 32 input features. Both data groups use the same corresponding weight slice.</td></tr>
      <tr data-placement="bias"><th scope="row">Bias</th><td><code>hidden=<span data-dim="hidden" data-split="model">64</span></code></td><td>Each model partition holds the bias for its 32 hidden columns. Both data groups use the same corresponding bias slice.</td></tr>
      <tr data-placement="output"><th scope="row">Output</th><td><code>batch=<span data-dim="batch" data-split="data">16</span>, hidden=<span data-dim="hidden" data-split="model">64</span></code></td><td>Each device produces a distinct block of eight batch rows and 32 hidden columns.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </details>
</div>
```

`platform.registerMesh` maps the logical axes to the **physical mesh**, the
devices and their connections. `Shape.withPartitioning` assigns tagged
dimensions to those axes. A `.replicated` dimension stays whole on each shard.
Inside `forward`, `Tensor.withPartitioning` constrains the placement of a graph value.
See the [tagged-shape guide]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/tensors-and-shapes')) and the
[sharding example source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/sharding/main.zig#L204-L236).

# [Run the sharding demo]($heading.id('run-the-sharding-demo'))

Start with automatic topology discovery. The default partitioner at this
revision is Shardy. The program also accepts `--partitioner=gspmd` to select
the GSPMD partitioner.

```sh
bazel run //examples/sharding -- --mesh=auto
```

Run on CPU with a mock topology to see data and model partitions on a
simulated eight-device grid.

```sh
bazel run //examples/sharding -- --mesh=mock --partitioner=shardy
```

Compare the physical topology, logical coordinates, and placement of the
input, weights, and bias in the logs. The demo applies a dense layer followed
by a per-shard computation and prints `output preview` with up to 16 values.
The discovered topology determines the actual split sizes.

# [Run data-parallel MNIST]($heading.id('run-data-parallel-mnist'))

```sh
bazel run //examples/mnist:mnist_dp
```

Bazel supplies the pretrained weights and test images. This variant fixes
`.data` to two groups and lets ZML infer the size of `.model`. It splits a
batch of four images so each data group receives two. When `.model` has
more than one device per group, the first layer is partitioned along its
output dimension and the second along its input dimension. The second
layer's bias is replicated.

Look for the printed mesh, a weight-transfer message, and four
`Shard … RECOGNIZED DIGIT` entries. Each entry is labeled by its index in
the batch. If you enable an accelerator backend, the available device
topology must support a data axis of size two. Use the CPU commands above
to try the examples on a machine with a single accelerator.
See [Bazel backend flags]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/build-and-run-with-bazel').ref('choose-a-backend-and-target-platform'))
and the [MNIST source](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/examples/mnist/mnist_data_parallel.zig).

The commands and expected output follow the pinned source. Device execution
checks are pending.
